---
import Gallery from '../../layouts/Gallery.astro';
import Related from '../../components/Related.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<Gallery title="Pattern: kanban board" description="Seeing where a batch of things stands all at once, as items move through stages — production orders, shipments, a queue with columns.">
  <p class="demo-note">
    <strong>Who uses it</strong>: whoever needs to see WHERE a batch of
    things stands, all at once — a production planner watching orders move
    through stages, a fulfilment lead watching shipments. <strong>How
    often</strong>: a screen left open, glanced at through a shift.
    <strong>What "done" looks like</strong>: every card in the lane that
    matches its real state, and moving one is a single deliberate act.
    <strong>Not for finding one specific record</strong> — that is
    <a href={base + '/patterns/list-report'}>list report</a>, grouped by
    status if stage is what matters; a board earns its place only when the
    SHAPE of stages side-by-side is the thing being read, not any one
    row's fields. <strong>Not drag-and-drop</strong> — moving a card here
    is a menu action, not a drag gesture; see why below.
  </p>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Why no drag, in this baseline</h2>
    <p>A card's stage is changed through <strong>Move to…</strong> in its
    own action menu, never by dragging. This is the baseline verdict from
    the pattern's own grill (100.1): drag has no accessible substitute that
    has ever stabilized — the ARIA properties written for it
    (<code>aria-grabbed</code>, <code>aria-dropeffect</code>) were
    deprecated in ARIA 1.1 for being unreliably implemented, so a real drag
    implementation means hand-rolling a second interaction model (pick up /
    move / drop + live-region narration) and maintaining it forever
    alongside this one. The menu already gives every card a keyboard path:
    <kbd>Enter</kbd> on a card's <strong>Move&nbsp;▾</strong> button opens
    the popover and <kbd>Tab</kbd> walks its items, which is more than a
    drag has ever offered.</p>
    <p><strong>It is not a glove-sized target, and this page claimed it
    was</strong> — corrected in Slice 319 after the clause was measured on
    the built page rather than reasoned about. Each trigger here is
    <code>bo-btn--sm</code>, whose <code>--bo-btn-height</code> is a fixed
    <code>1.5rem</code>: it is 24px in <em>every</em> density tier,
    <code>spacious</code> included, so no density setting grows it. That is
    the WCAG 2.5.8 minimum met through the spacing exception
    (<a href={base + '/concepts/density'}>density</a> explains the test),
    not the 44px control this framework calls the glove tier. A board that
    really is worked in gloves drops <code>--sm</code> on the trigger; the
    menu items themselves are already 37px tall.</p>
    <p><strong>The announcement is the one thing the menu does NOT give
    you</strong> — corrected here in Slice 317, because this paragraph used
    to claim it did. A native button in a popover is operable and silent:
    nothing tells a screen-reader user that the card moved. That channel is
    yours to wire, and the reason it cannot be handed to you is measured
    rather than asserted — a spike (roadmap 300.2) drove one generic
    keyboard-move core against two boards with different workflows and
    found that <strong>four of the five things it could not know were
    announcement strings</strong>: the domain noun, the workflow vocabulary
    of a refusal, and which facts even matter (a destination alone, or a
    destination and a position, where position is priority). The Data
    contract below names the channel; what it says is the screen's call,
    the same way the legal transitions are.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>The board</h2>
    <div class="bo-cluster" role="group" aria-label="Production orders by stage" tabindex="0" style="align-items: flex-start; gap: var(--bo-space-4); overflow-x: auto; padding-block-end: var(--bo-space-2)">
