    <script>
      import { initDropdowns } from '@busy-office/ui/js';
      initDropdowns();
    </script>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Every lane's count is a
    <a href={base + '/components/badge'}>badge</a> in its header — the
    monitoring signal, readable without opening a single card. A card
    ageing in one lane (Blocked, "since 09:40") is visible the same way an
    <a href={base + '/patterns/inbox'}>inbox</a> row's age is: text, not
    colour alone.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Markup</h2>
    <pre><code>{`<!-- One lane. Widget + a plain list of cards; each card's move
     action is a dropdown menu, not a drag handle. -->
<section class="bo-widget">
  <div class="bo-widget__header">
    <span class="bo-widget__title">Queued</span>
    <span class="bo-badge">3</span>          <!-- the monitoring signal -->
  </div>
  <div class="bo-widget__body">
    <ul class="bo-stack bo-stack--tight docs-list-bare">
      <li class="bo-form-section">
        <p><strong>WO-4471</strong> · Hydraulic pump assy</p>
        <p class="bo-byline bo-byline--compact">Line 2 · due 28 Aug</p>
        <button class="bo-btn bo-btn--sm bo-btn--secondary"
                type="button" popovertarget="kb-menu-1">Move ▾</button>
        <div class="bo-dropdown__menu" id="kb-menu-1" popover>
          <button class="bo-dropdown__item" type="button">Move to In progress</button>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<script type="module">
  import { initDropdowns } from "@busy-office/ui/js";
  initDropdowns();
</script>`}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Anatomy</h2>
    <ol class="bo-stack bo-stack--tight docs-list">
      <li><strong>Lane</strong> — a
      <a href={base + '/components/dashboard'}>widget</a>, one per stage,
      laid out in a horizontally-scrolling
      <a href={base + '/base/primitives'}>cluster</a>. A lane's count badge
      is the monitoring signal for that stage.</li>
      <li><strong>Card</strong> — a plain
      <a href={base + '/components/form'}>form-section</a> box reused as
      card chrome; identity + the one or two facts that matter for this
      board (due date, blocked reason).</li>
      <li><strong>Move action</strong> — a
      <a href={base + '/components/dropdown'}>dropdown</a> listing only
      the stages this card can legally move to next — never every stage,
      which would let a card skip steps a real process cannot skip.</li>
      <li><strong>Done lane</strong> — cards lose their action; a completed
      card is a record, not a thing to act on again.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Data contract</h2>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Request</th><th scope="col">Returns / on failure</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">GET /board</td><td>every lane's cards + counts in one response — an agent reads board state the same way the human sees it, no separate API</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">POST /cards/:id/move</td><td><code>{'{'} to: "in_progress" {'}'}</code> — the card re-rendered in its new lane, both counts updated; 409 if the target stage is not legal from the current one (re-render the card where it is, with the reason)</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">legal transitions</td><td>the SERVER decides which "Move to…" items a card's menu offers — the client never guesses the workflow, it renders what it was sent</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">move announcement</td><td>the screen owns a <code>role="status" aria-live="polite"</code> node (visually hidden) beside the board, and writes the result of every move into it — both the accepted move and the 409. The framework does not supply the sentence: it is the domain noun, your workflow's words for a refusal, and which facts matter on THIS board (destination alone, or destination and position where position is priority)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><strong>The AI-manages / human-monitors
    split, stated</strong>: an agent moves cards through the identical
    POST a human's menu item calls — no separate automation path. The
    human's channel is the lane's own shape: card counts, which lane is
    growing, which card has aged in Blocked. That is the whole reason a
    board earns its place over a grouped list — the shape is read at a
    glance, and it must stay true for an agent's moves exactly as much as
    a human's.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>States</h2>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">State</th><th scope="col">What ships</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Empty lane</td><td>"No orders queued" text inside the lane — an empty widget body reads as broken, not empty</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Empty board</td><td>the DIFFERENT empty — every lane shows nothing because nothing exists yet, distinct from a filter excluding everything</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Move accepted</td><td>the card re-renders in its new lane and both counts change — that is the whole visible signal, and a screen-reader user gets none of it unless the move result is written into the status node the Data contract names</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Move rejected (409)</td><td>the card stays in its lane, states why ("Cannot skip Blocked — resolve the shortage first") — in the status node, not only as a visual toast: a refusal a keyboard user cannot hear reads as the menu item having done nothing</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Card aged past a threshold</td><td>the byline gains a warning word ("Blocked 6h") — two-channel, same as the job monitor's stalled state</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Very long lane</td><td>the lane scrolls internally past a height, never grows the whole board vertically past the viewport</td></tr>
          <tr><td>No JS</td><td>the Move menu still opens — <code>popovertarget</code> is the native HTML Popover API, so a card's menu button works with no script at all. Without <code>initDropdowns()</code> the menu just isn't anchored to its button (no positioning at our browser floor, so it opens in the popover's default top-layer position rather than under the trigger) and it stays open after a click instead of auto-closing on selection. The "Move to…" items themselves have no click handler in this static demo either way — a real build would need a form/link per item for the move to complete without JS</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Focus after a move the server accepted — or refused</h2>
    <p><strong>The board above is static.</strong> Its <em>Move&nbsp;▾</em>
    menus open and close for real, and that is all: no menu item has a handler,
    nothing moves between lanes, and nothing is restored afterwards. What
    follows describes what <strong>your</strong> build must do once a real
    <code>POST /cards/:id/move</code> comes back — it is not a description of
    this demo.</p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">The problem is specific to this pattern: the
    control the user activated lives <em>inside the card</em>, and a successful
    move re-renders that card in a different lane. The menu they were in is
    replaced, its trigger with it — so by the time the response lands, the
    element that had focus is detached and focus is on
    <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>.</p>
    <ol class="bo-stack bo-stack--tight docs-list">
      <li><strong>Accepted (2xx).</strong> The card is re-rendered in its new
      lane. Find it again <strong>by its card identity</strong> — the
      <code>:id</code> you just posted to — and focus its new
      <em>Move&nbsp;▾</em> trigger, so the user lands on the same card they
      moved, now in its new place. Never re-find it by lane position: the
      position is what changed.</li>
      <li><strong>Refused (409).</strong> The data contract above re-renders
      the card <em>where it is</em>, with the reason. Focus its trigger again —
      the one in the card that did not move — so the user is back where they
      acted, next to the explanation. Do not focus the target lane: nothing
      arrived there.</li>
      <li><strong>The card or its action is gone.</strong> A move can legally
      leave a card with no action at all — the Done lane's cards have no
      trigger, by design, because a completed card is a record and not a thing
      to act on again. If the re-render leaves no trigger on that card, focus
      the card itself; if the card is absent entirely, focus its lane's
      heading. <strong>Both of those need <code>tabindex="-1"</code></strong> —
      a heading is no more focusable than a list item, and
      <code>.focus()</code> on either is a no-op without it. Name a fallback
      for each step;
      the failure mode here is silently landing on <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>
      after a successful action.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><strong>Only when the moved card held focus —
    and read that from the OLD card, immediately before you replace it.</strong>
    A board is a shared screen: someone else's move, or a poll, can re-render a
    card while this user is reading or typing elsewhere, and placing focus then
    is an interruption rather than a courtesy. The check has to happen while the
    old card is still in the document — testing the <em>new</em> card after
    rendering asks about an element that never had focus, and a flag saved when
    the request started goes stale if the user moves on while it is in flight.
    Capture it at the last moment before the swap, and act on that.</p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><strong>Which moves are offered stays the
    server's call</strong>, exactly as the Data contract says: the menu lists
    only the legal next stages, and a 409 is the server declining the move it
    was sent. That does not imply the client was wrong to offer it — on a
    shared board an offer that was legal when the menu rendered can be stale by
    the time it is activated, because someone else moved the card first. Treat
    409 as the current answer, re-render with the reason, and let the refreshed
    menu carry whatever is legal now. Focus handling does not change any of
    that, and must not be used to guess a workflow.</p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><strong>And focus is still not the
    announcement.</strong> As the section above this one already says, a native
    button in a popover is operable and silent — moving focus tells an
    assistive technology where the user now is, not that a card changed lane.
    &ldquo;WO-4471 moved to In progress, 3 in that lane&rdquo; is a sentence
    you write, into your own <a href={base + '/concepts/accessibility#live-regions'}>live
    region</a>, from the same handler that re-renders. Nothing on this page
    establishes what a screen reader actually says.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Why there is no drag here, and what would change that</h2>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Dragging cards between lanes has been refused
    more than once, on recorded evidence rather than taste. The refusals are
    not reopened here; they are quoted so a reader can see the bar rather than
    guess at it.</p>
    <ul class="bo-stack bo-stack--tight docs-list">
      <li><strong>Roadmap 100.1</strong> — the bar is
      <em>&ldquo;a specific screen, a specific row count, a user complaint
      about click count&rdquo;</em>. Note what it says next: on reopening, the
      first thing to examine is <strong>jump controls, not drag</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Roadmap 110.7</strong> — re-grilled the refusal at full
      depth and upheld it, explicitly excluding one kind of input:
      <em>not &ldquo;the owner wants it&rdquo; a third time</em>.</li>
      <li><strong>Roadmap 132.5</strong> — states no reopen sentence; its own
      Accept is the bar — name the shipped screen that needs it, and
      <em>if none exists, refuse and record it</em>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">The practical consequence for this page is the
    one already made above: the move affordance is a
    <a href={base + '/components/dropdown'}>menu</a>, every card is reachable
    by keyboard through it, and there is deliberately no drop-target styling
    because there is nothing to drop. A drag implementation would also owe
    <a href={base + '/reference/acr'}>WCAG 2.2 SC 2.5.7</a> a single-pointer
    alternative that does not require dragging — which is what the menu
    already is.</p>
  </section>

